(493, 11, 15, '<br>An Internship Experience at a Hotel<br>Last month, Dimas, a hospitality student, completed a five-day internship at Grand Palace Hotel. On the first day, he learned how to welcome guests and answer telephone calls. On the second and third days, he assisted the housekeeping team in preparing guest rooms. <br>On the fourth day, Dimas helped the receptionist check guests in and out under supervision. On the final day, his supervisor evaluated his performance. Dimas received 85 points for communication skills and 90 points for discipline. <br> The word &ldquo;evaluated&rdquo; in the text is closest in meaning to ....', 1, ' Ignored ', ' Invited ', ' Assessed ', ' Replaced ', ' Instructed', 'C', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(494, 11, 16, '<br>How to Prepare a Hotel Room<br>Preparing a hotel room properly is important to ensure guest comfort and safety. <br>1.Open the curtains and check the room&#39;s general condition. <br>2.Remove used towels and bed linen. <br>3.Clean the bathroom, including the toilet, sink, and shower. <br>4.Make the bed with clean linen and replace the towels. <br>5.Check the television, air conditioner, lights, and telephone. <br>6.Refill toiletries and drinking water. <br>7.Inspect the room before reporting it as ready. <br>A standard room takes approximately 25 minutes to prepare. <br> What is the purpose of the text?', 1, ' To describe the history of housekeeping ', ' To compare different types of hotel rooms ', ' To explain how to prepare a hotel room ', ' To persuade guests to clean their own rooms ', ' To explain how to check in at a hotel', 'C', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(495, 11, 17, '<br>How to Prepare a Hotel Room<br>Preparing a hotel room properly is important to ensure guest comfort and safety. <br>1.Open the curtains and check the room&#39;s general condition. <br>2.Remove used towels and bed linen. <br>3.Clean the bathroom, including the toilet, sink, and shower. <br>4.Make the bed with clean linen and replace the towels. <br>5.Check the television, air conditioner, lights, and telephone. <br>6.Refill toiletries and drinking water. <br>7.Inspect the room before reporting it as ready. <br>A standard room takes approximately 25 minutes to prepare. <br> What should housekeeping staff do after removing used towels and bed linen?', 1, ' Refill the drinking water. ', ' Clean the bathroom. ', ' Report the room as ready. ', ' Check the telephone. ', ' Open the curtains.', 'B', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(496, 11, 18, '<br>How to Prepare a Hotel Room<br>Preparing a hotel room properly is important to ensure guest comfort and safety. <br>1.Open the curtains and check the room&#39;s general condition. <br>2.Remove used towels and bed linen. <br>3.Clean the bathroom, including the toilet, sink, and shower. <br>4.Make the bed with clean linen and replace the towels. <br>5.Check the television, air conditioner, lights, and telephone. <br>6.Refill toiletries and drinking water. <br>7.Inspect the room before reporting it as ready. <br>A standard room takes approximately 25 minutes to prepare. <br> How long will it take to prepare four rooms if each room takes 25 minutes and they are prepared one after another?', 1, ' 50 minutes ', ' 75 minutes ', ' 90 minutes ', ' 100 minutes ', ' 125 minutes', 'D', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(497, 11, 19, '<br>How to Prepare a Hotel Room<br>Preparing a hotel room properly is important to ensure guest comfort and safety. <br>1.Open the curtains and check the room&#39;s general condition. <br>2.Remove used towels and bed linen. <br>3.Clean the bathroom, including the toilet, sink, and shower. <br>4.Make the bed with clean linen and replace the towels. <br>5.Check the television, air conditioner, lights, and telephone. <br>6.Refill toiletries and drinking water. <br>7.Inspect the room before reporting it as ready. <br>A standard room takes approximately 25 minutes to prepare. <br> Why should staff check the television, air conditioner, lights, and telephone?', 1, ' To ensure that the room&#39;s equipment works properly ', ' To calculate the hotel&#39;s room rates ', ' To decide which guest will stay there ', ' To reduce the number of hotel employees ', ' To decorate the room with new furniture', 'A', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(498, 11, 20, '<br>How to Prepare a Hotel Room<br>Preparing a hotel room properly is important to ensure guest comfort and safety. <br>1.Open the curtains and check the room&#39;s general condition. <br>2.Remove used towels and bed linen. <br>3.Clean the bathroom, including the toilet, sink, and shower. <br>4.Make the bed with clean linen and replace the towels. <br>5.Check the television, air conditioner, lights, and telephone. <br>6.Refill toiletries and drinking water. <br>7.Inspect the room before reporting it as ready. <br>A standard room takes approximately 25 minutes to prepare. <br> What is the final step in preparing the room?', 1, ' Remove the used bed linen. ', ' Clean the bathroom. ', ' Replace the towels. ', ' Refill the toiletries. ', ' Inspect the room before reporting it as ready.', 'E', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(499, 11, 21, '<br>Martha Matilda Harper<br>Martha Matilda Harper was an entrepreneur who helped develop the modern hairdressing industry. Born in Canada in 1857, she worked as a domestic servant when she was young. During this period, she learned about hair care and became interested in creating better hair treatments. <br>In 1888, Harper opened a hair salon in Rochester, New York. She later developed a business model that allowed other women to operate salons using her methods and products. Her system included training, consistent service standards, and a network of independently operated salons. <br>Harper&#39;s approach created opportunities for women to become business owners at a time when such opportunities were limited. <br> What is the text mainly about?', 1, ' The history of Rochester ', ' The development of hair products in Canada ', ' The daily activities of domestic servants ', ' The life and business achievements of Martha Matilda Harper ', ' The differences between hotels and beauty salons', 'D', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(500, 11, 22, '<br>Martha Matilda Harper<br>Martha Matilda Harper was an entrepreneur who helped develop the modern hairdressing industry. Born in Canada in 1857, she worked as a domestic servant when she was young. During this period, she learned about hair care and became interested in creating better hair treatments. <br>In 1888, Harper opened a hair salon in Rochester, New York. She later developed a business model that allowed other women to operate salons using her methods and products. Her system included training, consistent service standards, and a network of independently operated salons. <br>Harper&#39;s approach created opportunities for women to become business owners at a time when such opportunities were limited. <br> What did Harper do in 1888?', 1, ' She became a domestic servant. ', ' She opened a hair salon in Rochester. ', ' She moved from Canada to England. ', ' She closed her business network. ', ' She began working as a hotel receptionist.', 'B', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(501, 11, 23, '<br>Martha Matilda Harper<br>Martha Matilda Harper was an entrepreneur who helped develop the modern hairdressing industry. Born in Canada in 1857, she worked as a domestic servant when she was young. During this period, she learned about hair care and became interested in creating better hair treatments. <br>In 1888, Harper opened a hair salon in Rochester, New York. She later developed a business model that allowed other women to operate salons using her methods and products. Her system included training, consistent service standards, and a network of independently operated salons. <br>Harper&#39;s approach created opportunities for women to become business owners at a time when such opportunities were limited. <br> How many years passed between Harper&#39;s birth in 1857 and the opening of her salon in 1888?', 1, ' 21 years ', ' 25 years ', ' 28 years ', ' 30 years ', ' 31 years', 'E', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(502, 11, 24, '<br>Martha Matilda Harper<br>Martha Matilda Harper was an entrepreneur who helped develop the modern hairdressing industry. Born in Canada in 1857, she worked as a domestic servant when she was young. During this period, she learned about hair care and became interested in creating better hair treatments. <br>In 1888, Harper opened a hair salon in Rochester, New York. She later developed a business model that allowed other women to operate salons using her methods and products. Her system included training, consistent service standards, and a network of independently operated salons. <br>Harper&#39;s approach created opportunities for women to become business owners at a time when such opportunities were limited. <br> Which factor contributed to Harper&#39;s business growth?', 1, ' Avoiding employee training ', ' Offering only one service ', ' Using consistent standards and training ', ' Depending entirely on government funding ', ' Refusing to share business methods', 'C', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(503, 11, 25, '<br>Martha Matilda Harper<br>Martha Matilda Harper was an entrepreneur who helped develop the modern hairdressing industry. Born in Canada in 1857, she worked as a domestic servant when she was young. During this period, she learned about hair care and became interested in creating better hair treatments. <br>In 1888, Harper opened a hair salon in Rochester, New York. She later developed a business model that allowed other women to operate salons using her methods and products. Her system included training, consistent service standards, and a network of independently operated salons. <br>Harper&#39;s approach created opportunities for women to become business owners at a time when such opportunities were limited. <br> The word &ldquo;independently&rdquo; in the text is closest in meaning to ....', 1, ' Separately ', ' Carelessly ', ' Temporarily ', ' Unsuccessfully ', ' Secretly', 'A', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(504, 11, 26, '<br>Why Vocational Students Should Learn Digital Skills<br>Vocational students should develop digital skills because many workplaces now depend on technology. Employees in hotels, restaurants, offices, and retail businesses use digital systems to record transactions, communicate with customers, and manage information. <br>First, digital skills can improve work efficiency. An electronic booking system, for example, can help hotel staff manage reservations more quickly than a paper-based system. Second, digital competence can improve employment opportunities. Third, students who understand digital technology may be better prepared to adapt when workplace systems change. <br>Therefore, vocational schools should integrate digital learning into practical activities. <br> What is the writer&#39;s main argument?', 1, ' All vocational students should become programmers. ', ' Digital technology should replace teachers. ', ' Paper-based systems are always better than digital systems. ', ' Digital skills should be developed by vocational students. ', ' Students should avoid learning communication skills.', 'D', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
